E=[('Father and mother: moved to Chapter 11', 302, 'the family learns to take.\n\nMany families know the pattern behind these examples: a father who leans toward justice, a mother who leans toward mercy. This is part of how God made us. The Church teaches that the "difference and complementarity" of man and woman "are oriented toward the goods of marriage and the flourishing of family life" (CCC 2333). Each needs the other. His justice without her mercy crushes a child; her mercy without his justice spoils one. Held together, they show a child what God\'s love looks like, for in our Father in heaven, justice and mercy are one.', 'the family learns to take.'), ('A recap that repeats the four paragraphs', 304, 'Remove any one and the others give way with it—truth without mercy expels, mercy without truth enables, justice without sacrifice finds no one willing to bear its cost, sacrifice without justice is merely exploited.', 'Remove any one and the others give way with it.'), ('Call to Sainthood: a list the chapter already gave', 339, "They're being told that gender is a choice, that marriage is whatever you want it to be, that children don't need mothers and fathers, that life in the womb isn't really life, that the elderly and disabled are burdens, that truth is whatever you feel it to be.\n\n", '')]
